;;; tur/forms/stm -- software transactional memory: stm blocks and retry.
;;;
;;; A transaction reads and writes `TVar`s (tur/stm: `tvar/new`, `tvar/read`,
;;; `tvar/write`, `tvar/swap`, `tvar/cas`, `tvar/modify`) as if it were alone.
;;; `(atomically (stm ...))` runs it: the writes commit together, or -- if another
;;; thread committed to something the transaction read -- the attempt is thrown
;;; away and the body runs again. There are no locks to take and no deadlock, but
;;; the body can run more than once, so keep effects such as printing out of it.
;;; stm-guide has the mechanics, stm-tutorial the walkthrough.
;;;
;;; The `tvar/*` forms are accepted only lexically inside an `stm` block
;;; (TUR-E0009). A function that touches a TVar must contain its own `stm`, even
;;; if every caller already holds one. A value read from a TVar is a pointer-sized
;;; word: ascribe it, `(:: (tvar/read tv) :int)`. Floats work too.
;; Reference only: tools/gendocs.py reads the ;;@ lines, the compiler skips them.

;;; stm -- a transactional block: the body that `atomically` runs.
;;;
;;; Evaluates its forms in order and yields the last one's value, like `do`, but
;;; inside it the `tvar/*` forms work and see one consistent snapshot. Put
;;; `stm` directly under `atomically`, or in a function that is called from an
;;; `atomically` body. An `stm` nested in a running transaction joins it: its
;;; writes belong to the same commit. A nested `atomically` is refused at compile
;;; time (TUR-E0009): the inner block would be a transaction of its own that
;;; commits first and cannot see the outer one's uncommitted writes. An `stm`
;;; run with no `atomically` around it at all stops the program (`stm used outside
;;; of an atomically block`; the interpreter says `tvar/read used outside of an
;;; atomically block`). Do not `set!` a dynamic variable inside one (TUR-E0605): a
;;; retry cannot undo it.
;;;
;;; Parameters:
;;;   body -- the forms of the transaction
;;;
;;; Returns:
;;;   The value of the last form, once the transaction has committed.
;;;
;;; Example:
;;;   (atomically (stm 1 2 3))  ; => 3
;;;   (let [tv (tvar/new 42)] (:: (atomically (stm (tvar/read tv))) :int))  ; => 42
;;@form (stm body ...)

;;; retry -- abandon this attempt and run the transaction again later.
;;;
;;; Used to wait for a condition: `(when (= (:: (tvar/read flag) :int) 0) (retry))`.
;;; The runtime parks the thread until another transaction commits to something
;;; this one has read (no busy-waiting), then runs the body again from the top.
;;; If nothing ever does, the compiled program waits forever; the interpreter,
;;; which cannot wait on another thread, stops with `transaction requested retry
;;; with no way to make progress`. `retry` ends the attempt at once: the forms after
;;; it do not run. A retry that has read no TVar can never be woken, so the
;;; compiled program stops with the interpreter's message instead of spinning.
;;; Prefer `check`, which is `retry` unless a condition holds. `retry` has type
;;; `nil`, so both arms of an `if` around it must be `nil`. `or-else` tries an
;;; alternative instead of waiting.
;;;
;;; Example:
;;;   (atomically (stm (or-else (stm (retry) 1) (stm 2))))  ; => 2
;;@form (retry)
